                                                "sense_id": "1744c460-ec69-3607-90bc-f2a5b1ae2aca",
                                                "text": "1994, Mary C. Smith and David M. Sherman, Goat Medicine, Blackwell Publishing, →ISBN, page 429,\nThe newborn doe kids destined to become habitual aborters (and the buckling that carries the trait) are above average in weight and have a very fine haircoat."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d5c4f865-9ad9-4cf7-9213-553c132eb6b4",
                                                "sense_id": "1744c460-ec69-3607-90bc-f2a5b1ae2aca",
                                                "text": "1997, Ruth Schubarth, “Born Backwards”, in Linda M. Hasselstrom, Gaydell M. Collier, and Nancy Curtis (eds.), Leaning Into the Wind: Women Write from the Heart of the West, Houghton Mifflin Books, →ISBN, page 161,\nI milk the goats and put wethers (the castrated bucklings) in the freezer with ducks, chickens, rabbits, and lambs."
                                            }
